|> Exercise
    handle=_difference_quotient_illustration_

    The fraction

    $$
    {f(x+h) - f(x) \over h}
    $$

    where $f : \rr \ra \rr$ is called a _difference
    quotient_. Add the numerator and denominator to the diagram below to explain the quotient’s geometric meaning.

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch4_newton_quotient_unfinished.svg
        style=margin-top:0.4em

    |> Solution

        The teacher would like to see:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch4_newton_quotient_finished.svg

        Thus

        $$
        {f(x + h) - f(x)\over h}
        $$

        has the form _rise over run_: the slope of the pale brown line through

        $$
        (x, f(x))
        $$

        and

        $$
        (x + h, f(x + h)).
        $$

        !! |> ImageRight
        !!     offset-y=-0.5em
        !!     src=images/svg_ch4_new_quatient_x_fxplush_cloud.svg

        !! (This is also the case
        !! if $h$ is negative, by the way.)

        !! |> Pause
            
        !! _Vocabulary #1._ A fraction of this form is called
        !! a _difference quotient_.

        |> Pause

        _Vocabulary._
        The pale brown line is sometimes known as
        the _secant_ [through $(x, f(x))$, $(x+h, f(x+h))$].
        “Secant” is a general term for “line passing
        through two specified points on another
        curve”.

        |> SolutionNote
            handle=_difference_quotient_limit_
            If we let $h$ drop to $0$, and if $f$ is
            differentiable at $x$, the
            difference-quotient-a.k.a.-slope-of-the-secant
            approaches

            $$
            f'(x)
            $$

            because the secant approaches the tangent, and its slope approaches the tangent’s slope, $f'(x)$. 
            (But you
            cannot directly set $h = 0$, because

            $$
            {f(x+0)-f(x)\over 0} = {0 \over 0}
            $$

            is undefined.)
